mmunications and other  methods to be able to command and  control this force as a government  instrumentality and all of this is part  of an asymmetric law fair or asymmetric  tool of the government in order to  occupy and dominate the so-called gray  zone or this area that's uncomfortably  lying between peace and war during  wartime  the militia can can conduct a number of  different functions or missions  including intelligence surveillance and  reconnaissance concealment harassment  blockade envelopment and minesweeping  transportation island defense and  patrols and the militia is not a new  it's not a not a new event in fact after  World War two it was used primarily to  counter nationalist infiltration of the  mainland and then in 1974 it was  instrumental in the Battle of the  Paracel Islands in seizing the Paracel  Islands from Vietnam and this battle has  now been mythologized as as the first  sort of successful naval battle and also  the first successful major operation of  this combined civil and military force  to achieve the aims of the state right  now we're witnessing the maritime  militia involved in a number of  peacetime roles including at the core of  the so called cabbage strategy or gray  zone dominance also critical  infrastructure protection such as ports  and oil rigs and certainly rights  protection missions or missions to  vindicate or advance the the the claimed  rights of China primarily based upon  historic rights harassment of four  vessels foreign vessels that are both  warships as well as civilian vessels  again is our including this instant in  incident here where a fishing vessel was  able to net a some countries on unmanned  underwater vehicle and then deliver it  to Chinese authorities there's also now  growing suspicion that the maritime  militia may be involved in interference  with submarine cables as well as of  course what we're most familiar with  which is its role in power projection  I'll focus on just one of these  cooperatives which is the Sagnier Fugen  fisheries company and it was founded in  2001 and it's quickly grown since then  it it launched in 2011 a 3,000 tonne  fishery supply and command and control  ship that it's used or operated in  conjunction with fisheries vessels to to  project power and to harass foreign  ships so in 2010 for example this large  command and control ship led a flotilla  of 29 vessels around a number of the  features in the vicinity of the Spratly  Islands including fiery cross reef subi  reef mischief reef and operating in  formation steaming and with command and  control from the large ship and then  upon their return they went through  recognition ceremonies arrival  ceremonies citations for bravery on  behalf of the state this is the same  group by the way that was involved in  the in the hys y98 one cabbage tactics  or cabbage strategy to protect that  critical asset that was placed in the  disputed waters and that was involved  with Vietnam drove away a number of  Vietnam Vietnamese vessels that were  protesting the arrival of this oil rig  in Viet Nam's exclusive economic zone  and this is also the group that was  involved in two other incidents the Binh  Minh 2 and the Viking 2 in 2011 it's  going on even today just about a month  ago via  seized a vessel that was disguised as a  fishing boat but in fact was a tanker  with a hundred thousand litres of diesel  fuel on board that was being used to  supply illicitly supply fuel to fishing  vessels that were operating in Viet  Nam's exclusive economic zone the  Philippines has also been subject to  this same fishing cooperative maritime  militia tactics specifically and most  recently in second Thomas shoal in 2014  when this the 8161 the 3,000 tonne  command and control ship led seven large  phu gong trawlers to try to intercept  the resupply of the beer the BRP sierra  madre which is this derelict former US  warship that the philippines has  grounded on the reefs there at second  Thomas shoal in order to in order to  sort of resist a Chinese encroachment  also just a month ago or six weeks ago  or so  Malaysia experienced what appears to be  maritime militia when a hundred Chinese  fishing vessels were operating around  South laconia Shoals offshore of Sarawak  that far south and the Malaysia maritime  enforcement agency and the Royal  Malaysian Navy encountered all of these  fishing vessels that had no flags and no  apparent registration a hundred vessels  and yet they were operating in  conjunction with two Chinese Coast Guard  ships Indonesia as well again about six  weeks ago in Indonesia was involved with  the this incident the Quaife a one zero  zero seven eight and eight crew that  were arrested for illegal ship illegal  fishing in the Natuna islands EEZ and  the vessel towline was cut by Chinese  coast guard vessel these Indonesia the  Ministry of maritime affairs and  Fisheries has had several incidents  where it  where its operations against illegal  fishing illegal Chinese fishing have  been disrupted by Chinese Coast Guard  ships including ships that these  fisheries law enforcement vessels 310  and 311 that have actually pointed  weapons at the Indonesian vessels and  it's interesting that this these two  ships 310 and 311 continue to sort of  crop up in incidents in this one  incidents in March of 2013 the fisheries  law enforcement vessel 310 demanded the  release of captured fishermen that had  been fishing illegally and in that  instance it used sirens and bridge to  bridge communications as well as  apparently satellite communications  jamming equipment to prevent the  Indonesian forces from contacting their  headquarters and in that incident as  well as in all the incidents actually  Indonesia backed down until this most  recent one in 2016 so that adds a new  sort of cyber warfare wrinkle to the  maritime militia that apparently they're  capable in some instances of of  operating in tandem with the Coast Guard  and preventing communications between  but between other forces and their  national leaders it's not just the South  China Sea but it's also in the East  China Sea Korea has encountered Chinese  fishing vessels and and maritime militia  in this case you can see here in the  photograph that Chinese vessels have  lashed all of their ships together in  order to impede boarding by the Republic  of Korea Coast Guard Japan has faced  similar challenges around the waters of  the Senkaku Islands and of course the  United States on a number of occasions  has encountered vessels which have which  have confirmed ties to the maritime  militia particularly this trawler eighty  three ninety nine that you see here that  was involved in the USNS impecable  incident that was the 2009 incident when  the United States impeccable which is a  u.s. navy auxilary warship was  conducting military surveys  in the Chinese exclusive economic zone  and it was surrounded by government  vessels as well as trawlers that have  been that have been linked as members of  the maritime militia in an effort to try  to impede those military activities so  with that I'll turn now toward the legal  implications and with the legal  implications it's important to keep in  mind that there's two bodies of law that  would apply there's a there's a a law of  naval warfare that would a flood that  would apply during periods of armed  conflict and then there's the peacetime  law of the sea or the United Nations  Convention on the law of the sea which  sort of serves as the constitution for  the world's oceans and under that  peacetime regime vessels may may be  flagged in any state but whatever state  carries that flag they the the state has  a duty of implementing internationally  accepted standards and so what I would  argue or suggest is that the maritime  militia in practice so far has not  complied with these internationally  accepted standards so in peacetime it's  important to remember that in the UN  Convention on the law of the sea the  flag state has important rights as well  as corresponding duties so the flag  state has a right to register vessels  and have those vessels fly its flag and  then those ships are free to use the  global Commons and travel throughout the  world but flag states also have  corresponding duties which sometimes are  not as as strongly observed we see this  in the phenomenon of open registries or  flag states of convenience but it also  appears to be a feature of the maritime  militia where the flag state has failed  to exercise effective jurisdiction or  effective and exclusive control over the  maritime militia and has not observed  international rules and it has failed to  investigate incidents or cooperate with  other states to find out what has  occurred  and it is also failed to to monitor  administrative technical and social  matters and make sure that the crew are  trained and operate in accordance with  international standards  well what standards are we talking about  in particular there's the collision  regulations or the International  Convention for collision regulations and  I've highlighted just a couple of them  here as as I talked about it you can see  that this is a Chinese maritime militia  a cargo ship that's bearing down and  actually sank a Vietnamese fishing  vessel but the collision regulations  have a a number of internationally  accepted standards and those standards  apply both to warships as well as  merchant vessels and they include rules  six seven and eight which I've  highlighted here which is the obligation  or the duty to maintain a safe speed  rule seven is the duty to avert any sort  of risk of collision that is to take  affirmative steps to avoid collision and  then rule eight is even stronger  language which is that that every  possible means must be taken to avoid a  collision here you see apparently just  the opposite of that where a maritime  militia vessel actually sank a  Vietnamese fishing vessel there is some  suggestion that the maritime militia may  be involved in submarine cable cutting  there's less direct evidence but but I  think that it's probably it will  probably more will come to light in any  event merit submarine cables are  responsible for about 95 percent of  global telecommunications and of course  everything that we do from from banking  to communications is linked to submarine  cables it all goes under the water  rather than through satellite because  satellite is still far too expensive and  the bandwidth is limited China is by far  the global leader in what are called  politely cable faults which are which  are broken  broken submarine cables and the  principal responsibility lies with  Chinese fishing vessels so there may be  instances particularly in cases of  inadvertent dragged anchor where it  might be inadvertent although it also  could be a purposeful campaign in any  event it is somewhat telling that  there's not been a single civil case  against a Chinese fishing vessel for a  broken submarine cable even though they  are the principal agent for those  incidents if so this would be  inconsistent with a long-standing and  and multi-dimensional regime or legal  set of rules for submarine cables going  back to 1884 which says that all vessels  should stand clear of areas of submarine  cables including of vessels that are  that are repairing or that are  maintaining submarine cables so the  vessels that are maintaining and  repairing submarine cables actually have  a right of way under the collision  regulations and the UN Convention on the  law of the sea and other multilateral  conventions and those rules do not  appear to have been observed in several  instances that involve maritime militia  in the few minutes remaining I'll to  also talk about the law of naval warfare  and this is that body of law that  applies during periods of armed conflict  the problem that the militia pose is  that it violates one of the core  fundamental principles of the law of  armed conflict also known as  international humanitarian law and that  is that humanitarian law is designed to  protect civilians and civilian objects  such as regular workers schools  hospitals and also fishing vessels so  this is the principle of distinction  that is that states have a duty to make  these civilian objects distinct and  separate from its Armed Forces and  that's exactly the opposite of what a  maritime militia does which tends to  blur or merge the civilian forces into  the  the Armed Forces what this does is it  exploits a gap in international law  between peace and war and this gap was  really exposed in the US v Nicaragua  case at the International Court of  Justice that my dear colleague Nong  mentioned in in her presentation and  that is that under the UN Charter states  have a right of self-defense if they are  subject to an armed attack or in the  equally valid French translation armed  aggression so if a state commits armed  aggression which is forbidden and  crosses the line of this article to  paragraph four of the UN Charter a state  at some point thereafter enjoys the  right of self-defense but what the  Nicaragua case said is that it's not a  bright line in fact the u.s. lost that  case because the u.s. claimed that once  this line is crossed and that that a  state is subject to an armed attack that  immediately triggers this right of  self-defense the court disagreed and  said no there's actually this gap that  even though a country is subject to an  armed attack it may not exercise the  right of self-defense if that armed  attack does not reach a sufficient  gravity or have sufficient scale and  effects of a conventional attack what  this does in the in my view and in the  view of some scholars is that it creates  this gap which seems to license this  low-level asymmetric armed coercion or  armed attack and therefore it actually  is ripe for exploitation by a force such  as the maritime militia that comfortably  operates and dominates inside this gap  achieving its ends while avoiding  full-fledged conflict thank you very  much

